course: space
lesson: 20
title: Constellations and the night sky in winter and spring
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A constellation is an area of sky with an agreed pattern of stars, like a county on a map of the sky: astronomers use 88 official ones that together cover the whole sky.
explain: The evening sky changes through the year because the Earth goes round the Sun, so at night we face a different part of space each season, like children on a roundabout seeing different faces in the crowd as they go round.
explain: In winter, Orion the hunter is the easy one: three bright stars in a row make his belt, which points down-left to Sirius and up-right to orange Aldebaran in Taurus.
explain: All year from Britain or Canada the Plough never sets, and its two Pointers lead to Polaris, the Pole Star, almost still above due north, with the W of Cassiopeia on the other side.
explain: In spring Leo appears in the south with his backwards question mark, the Sickle; and the curve of the Plough's handle leads you to "arc to Arcturus" and "speed on to Spica".
fact: A constellation is an area of sky with an agreed pattern of stars.
fact: Since 1922 to 1930 astronomers have used 88 official constellations covering the whole sky.
fact: The evening constellations change through the year because the Earth moves round the Sun, so at night we face a different part of space each season; in summer Orion is up in the daytime sky, hidden by sunlight.
fact: Orion, the hunter, is the easiest winter constellation from Britain and North America, with three bright stars in a row making his belt.
fact: Orion's belt points down-left to Sirius, the brightest star, and up-right to orange Aldebaran in Taurus.
fact: Betelgeuse, Sirius and Procyon form the Winter Triangle, a large shape of three bright stars in the winter evening sky; it is not one of the 88 constellations but a shape made by stars from three of them.
fact: Taurus has the V-shaped Hyades and the Pleiades, and Gemini has the twin stars Castor and Pollux, both high on winter evenings in the north.
fact: The Plough, called the Big Dipper in North America, is part of Ursa Major and never sets from Britain or Canada, so it can be seen on any clear night of the year.
fact: The two end stars of the Plough's bowl, the Pointers, lead to Polaris, the Pole Star, which stays almost still above due north.
fact: Cassiopeia, a W or M of five stars, sits on the other side of Polaris from the Plough and is also always up from Britain.
fact: In spring, Leo the lion appears in the south, its head and mane a backwards question mark called the Sickle, with bright Regulus at the bottom.
fact: In spring, follow the curve of the Plough's handle to "arc to Arcturus" and then "speed on to Spica" in Virgo.
fact: The stars of a constellation only look close together from the Earth; most are at very different distances from us.
exercise: What is a constellation? | An area of sky with an agreed pattern of stars | A group of planets all going round the same star | A cloud of gas and dust where new stars are born | A single very bright star that has its own name
exercise: How many official constellations are there? | 88 | 12 | 8 | 1,000
exercise: Why do the evening constellations change through the year? | The Earth moves round the Sun, so at night we face a different part of space | The stars themselves travel round the Earth once a year, shifting a bit every night | Clouds cover different stars each season | The stars switch off in summer
exercise: Which is the easiest winter constellation to find from Britain and North America? | Orion, the hunter | Leo, the lion | Cassiopeia, the queen | Virgo, the maiden
exercise: What makes Orion's belt? | Three bright stars in a row | Two stars called the Pointers | A W of five stars | A backwards question mark
exercise: Follow Orion's belt down to the left. Which star do you reach? | Sirius | Polaris | Regulus | Arcturus
exercise: Follow Orion's belt up to the right. Which star do you reach? | Orange Aldebaran in Taurus | Spica in Virgo | Polaris in the north | Regulus in Leo
exercise: Which three stars make the Winter Triangle? | Betelgeuse, Sirius and Procyon | Castor, Pollux and Regulus | Arcturus, Spica and Polaris | Aldebaran, Altair and Vega
exercise: Which constellation has the V-shaped Hyades and the Pleiades? | Taurus | Gemini | Leo | Orion
exercise: Castor and Pollux are the twin stars of which constellation? | Gemini | Taurus | Ursa Major | Cassiopeia
exercise: What is the Plough called in North America? | The Big Dipper | The Little Bear | The Winter Triangle | The Sickle
exercise: When can you see the Plough from Britain or Canada? | On any clear night of the year | Only in winter | Only in summer | Only once every 76 years
exercise: What do the two Pointers of the Plough lead to? | Polaris, the Pole Star | Sirius, the brightest star | Orion's belt | The Moon
exercise: Why is Polaris so useful? | It stays almost still above due north | It is the brightest star of all in the night sky | It rises in the east at the same time every evening | It shows exactly where the Sun will set that night
exercise: What shape is Cassiopeia? | A W or M of five stars | A square with a long tail | Three stars in a row | A backwards question mark
exercise: What is the Sickle of Leo? | A backwards question mark making the lion's head and mane | The three stars of Orion's belt | The handle of the Plough | The V of the Hyades
exercise: Which bright star is at the bottom of the Sickle? | Regulus | Arcturus | Spica | Sirius
exercise: In spring, where does the curve of the Plough's handle lead? | Arc to Arcturus, then speed on to Spica | Arc to Aldebaran, then on to Sirius | Up to Polaris first and then across to Cassiopeia | Straight down to Orion's belt
test: My mate in Manchester says he can only see the Plough at Christmas. right? | No, from Britain it never sets, so any clear night will do | Yes, it only rises over Britain in the weeks around Christmas | No, it can only ever be seen from Australia and New Zealand | Yes, it is a winter-only pattern that hides all summer long
test: lost in the woods at night, which way's north?? | Find Polaris with the Plough's Pointers and face it | Face the brightest star you can see, that's always north | Walk towards Orion's belt | Follow the Moon wherever it is
test: In a game, Orion's belt is drawn as a W of five stars. What is wrong? | Orion's belt is three stars in a row; the W is Cassiopeia | Orion has no belt at all | Orion's belt is a backwards question mark, like Leo's head | Nothing, that is exactly right
test: Why can't you see Orion on a summer evening, but you can on a winter one? | In summer we face the other way at night, and Orion is in the daytime sky | Orion moves to another galaxy in summer | Orion only shines when the night air is cold, so in warm summer it fades out | Summer clouds always hide it
test: Lily says the stars of a constellation are all close together out in space, like a family. true? | No, they only look close from the Earth and lie at very different distances | Yes, they are all the same distance away, like dots painted on one big ball | Yes, they are joined by lines in space | No, they are all inside the Solar System
test: If there were no agreed list of constellations, what would be harder? | Telling people exactly which part of the sky you mean | Seeing the stars at night | Finding the Moon | Keeping the stars from moving
test: Which pattern is not one of the 88 official constellations but a shape made by stars from three of them? | The Winter Triangle | Orion | Leo | Taurus
test: You see a backwards question mark in the south on a spring evening. What are you looking at? | The Sickle of Leo | Orion's belt | The Plough's handle | Cassiopeia
test: this app says Big Dipper but my nan says the Plough. who's right, for god's sake | Both, they are two names for the same pattern | Only the app is right, Plough is just an old nickname | Only nan is right, Big Dipper is a made-up app name | Neither, it is really called the Sickle, after the farm tool
test: Which is true? | Cassiopeia is on the other side of Polaris from the Plough | Cassiopeia is part of Orion | Cassiopeia only rises in summer | Cassiopeia is the brightest single star in the northern sky
test: Betelgeuse and Aldebaran are both orange-red winter stars. Which of them is a corner of the Winter Triangle? | Betelgeuse | Aldebaran | Both of them | Neither of them
exam: State the number of constellations officially recognised by astronomers since the period nineteen hundred and twenty-two to nineteen hundred and thirty. | Eighty-eight | Twelve | One hundred | Eight
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the constellations visible in the evening sky vary in the course of a year. | The orbital motion of the Earth about the Sun | The motion of the stars about the Earth | The obscuration of the stars by seasonal clouds | The extinction of the stars during summer
exam: Ascertain the star to which the two terminal stars of the bowl of the Plough, designated the Pointers, are directed. | Polaris | Sirius | Arcturus | Regulus
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the three stars which constitute the Winter Triangle. | Betelgeuse, Sirius and Procyon | Castor, Pollux and Regulus | Arcturus, Spica and Polaris | Aldebaran, Altair and Vega
